// A container php::print_r cannot render yet must be refused BY ITS OWN NAME.
//
// This used to descend four levels into libstdc++'s red-black tree and refuse
// there:
//
//   php::print_r: member '_M_t': member '_M_impl': member '_M_header':
//   member '_M_color': no dumper for type '_Rb_tree_color' yet
//
// — naming a type the user never wrote and never once saying std::map. A
// std::map is not a positional sequence (its operator[] takes a KEY), so
// dump_sequence declines it and the member walk took over. The member walk is
// right for an ordinary aggregate and wrong for a container: those members are
// the library's internals, not the user's data.
//
// The gate is the MESSAGE. Removing the by-name refusal makes the diagnostic
// mention _Rb_tree_color again, which this fixture no longer matches.
//
// The message must also not overstate the gap. It used to end "its elements need
// the begin()/end() protocol", which reads as though madc cannot call begin() or
// end() — it can: a hand-written `for (std::map<int,int>::iterator it =
// m.begin(); it != m.end(); ++it)` compiles and runs. What is missing is a
// GENERATED loop, because madc's one shared "is this iterable" recognizer
// (class_index_iteration_protocol) only knows the positional size() +
// operator[] shape. The fixture pins that wording too.
#include <map>

std::map<int,int> m;
php::print_r(m);
